<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Rehabilitation hydration principles"><div><strong>Access can be the barrier</strong><span>A person may be able to drink but unable to reach, open, hold or refill a container independently.</span></div><div><strong>Swallowing safety comes first</strong><span>Dysphagia can make ordinary liquids unsafe or require a prescribed texture and supervised strategy.</span></div><div><strong>Recovery is multidisciplinary</strong><span>Hydration belongs alongside nutrition, mobility, medication review, speech-language care and medical management.</span></div></div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Hydration in rehabilitation</p><h2>A normal drinking task can become surprisingly complex after illness or injury</h2><p>Rehabilitation patients may face weakness, impaired coordination, upper-limb limitations, fatigue, pain, cognition changes, communication barriers or dependence on staff and caregivers for drinks. Stroke and neurologic illness can add dysphagia, which raises risks related to aspiration, nutrition and hydration.</p><p>That makes hydration an implementation problem as much as a beverage problem. The right cup, positioning, assistance, texture, timing and monitoring may matter more than the brand of drink.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/14797757/pexels-photo-14797757.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Physiotherapist assisting a patient with leg rehabilitation in a clinic" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Function and access</p><h2>Make drinking part of the environment, not an extra task</h2><p>For a patient with safe oral intake, practical changes can include placing drinks within reach, using containers the person can manage, offering drinks with therapy and meals, tracking intake when clinically indicated and involving the care team when barriers persist.</p><p>When swallowing is impaired, those general strategies must be adapted to the assessed swallowing plan. A standard thin beverage should never be promoted around a texture restriction simply because hydration is desirable.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Common barriers</p><h2>Why intake can fall during recovery</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4"><article><h3>Dysphagia</h3><p>Swallowing impairment may restrict liquid consistency or require therapeutic strategies and supervision.</p></article><article><h3>Mobility and dexterity</h3><p>Weakness, tremor, pain or one-sided impairment can make containers difficult to access or use.</p></article><article><h3>Cognition and communication</h3><p>Memory, attention, aphasia or delirium can interfere with requesting or remembering drinks.</p></article><article><h3>Medical complexity</h3><p>Fluid restrictions, medications, renal function and ongoing losses can make generic encouragement inappropriate.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid"><article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where Best Hydrate may fit</p><h2>Only when the patient's oral plan makes it appropriate</h2><p>For a patient who is cleared for the relevant liquid consistency and whose care plan permits the ingredients and fluid volume, a labelled hydration product may be considered as one beverage option.</p><p>Best Hydrate should not be marketed as a rehabilitation treatment, a dysphagia intervention or a substitute for prescribed fluid therapy. Its role must remain compatible with the person's swallowing, medical and nutrition plan.</p></article><aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Swallowing safety</span><h3>Do not override a texture prescription.</h3><p>Speech-language pathology and clinical swallowing recommendations take priority over ordinary beverage preparation.</p></aside></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Care pathway</p><h2>Connect hydration to the rest of recovery</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3"><article><h3>Clinical Development</h3><p>Research should define the population, comparator, safety and meaningful outcomes.</p><a href="/pages/clinical-development">Clinical Development →</a></article><article><h3>Active Aging</h3><p>Older adults can face overlapping thirst, mobility and medical barriers.</p><a href="/pages/active-aging">Active Aging →</a></article><article><h3>Healthcare Applications</h3><p>See the broader set of hydration questions across care settings.</p><a href="/pages/healthcare-applications">Healthcare Applications →</a></article></div></section>

